00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.068
[ELECTRONIC BEEPING]

00:00:25.265 --> 00:00:27.600
Welcome to Cloud AI
Adventures, where

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.650
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:34.440
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.690
And on this episode
of AI Adventures,

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:40.440
I'll show you how to get started
with Keras in the quickest way

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.490
possible.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.550
It's never been easier to
get started with Keras.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:47.160
Not only is Keras
built into TensorFlow

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.700
via tensorflow.keras,
you also don't even

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.310
have to install or
configure anything

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.710
if you use a tool
like Kaggle Kernels.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:59.190
All you need to do is create
your Kaggle account if needed

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:00.420
and sign in.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.200
Then you have access to all
that Keras has to offer.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.590
Keras also exists as
a standalone library,

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:10.710
but the TensorFlow version
has the exact same APIs

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:12.660
and some extra features.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.470
Let's head over to
my Kaggle Kernel,

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:18.270
where I'll show you how to get
started using Keras right now.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.060
In a previous episode, we
did some machine learning

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.310
on the dataset Fashion-MNST.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:27.080
It's a dataset of 10 different
types of fashionable items,

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.240
from pants and shirts
to shoes and handbags,

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.470
all presented in 28
by 28 pixel grayscale.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.610
Mmm, grayscale.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:40.460
Today, we'll do a similar
analysis using Keras.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:44.060
So to use Keras, we'll just
import TensorFlow like usual.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.250
These imports are
actually identical to what

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:47.730
we had before.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.990
And we'll pull in numpy,
and pandas, and natplotlib

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.650
just as we normally would.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:57.090
And we continue operating
kind of in the usual way.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.720
We'll pull in our
training and test CSVs

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.130
and load them up in
pandas and take a look

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.690
at what they look like.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.210
We've got our Label
column on the far left

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.830
with numbers from
zero through nine.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:09.900
And we have our Pixels--

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:15.000
pixel 1, 2, 3, all the
way up to pixel 784.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.010
I've got a function
here to preprocess

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:17.927
our data a little bit.

00:02:17.927 --> 00:02:19.384
It's similar to
what we had before.

00:02:19.384 --> 00:02:21.850
I've just cleaned it up and
made it a little more concise.

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:24.570
I'm pulling out the features
and dividing by 255,

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.730
so we normalize all
the grayscale values

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.010
to be between zero and one.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.380
And I'll pull out
the labels as well

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:35.790
and have them both be
represented as an numpy arrays.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:38.850
We use that function to pull
out our training and test

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.820
data from the data
frame and associate them

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.340
to explicit variables--
train_features

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.670
and train_labels,
test_features and test_labels.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:52.350
And we can see that the final
shape of these variables

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.450
are exactly as we would expect--

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:58.890
60,000 examples
with 784 columns.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.904
And then our labels are
just the 60,000 values.

00:03:01.904 --> 00:03:03.820
And we're going to take
a peek at one of them.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:06.630
This is our 20th
training_feature set.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:08.540
And some pixels
in the middle, we

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:12.015
can see that they're indeed
values between zero and one.

00:03:12.015 --> 00:03:13.620
And we can also visualize it.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.100
Here we have a shirt, and we
can see that it looks exactly

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:18.210
as you'd expect--

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:21.652
kind of grainy and grayscale.

00:03:21.652 --> 00:03:24.210
Now, with Keras in
this particular case,

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.799
we're going to need to
one-hot hot encode our data.

00:03:26.799 --> 00:03:29.090
And what that means is we're
going to take our training

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:35.120
labels, which used to be just
values like zero, three, seven,

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:36.560
and turn them into--

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.770
each of them-- into
an array of length 10.

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:44.690
All 10 values in the array will
be zeros except for one value.

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:46.750
That one value will be a 1.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:49.550
And so that's why it's
called one-hot encoding.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:51.620
Now, where is that one located?

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:56.790
It's going to be exactly the
number that it came from.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.020
So, for example, if
the value was seven,

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:01.160
the seventh zero will be a one.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:04.850
If the number was four, then
the fourth zero will be a one--

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:06.620
hence one-hot encoding.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:10.910
And so we'll run Keras
wtils.to_categorical,

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:13.820
which is a handy utility
function that will just do this

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:14.780
for us.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:17.000
And we'll observe that
the train labels have now

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.690
turned from 60,000
rows of numbers

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:24.400
to 60,000 rows with 10 columns.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.040
And we can see that indeed, in
that same example label that we

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:31.700
saw before now, the
zeroth index has a 1,

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:34.715
and everything else
remains a zero.

00:04:34.715 --> 00:04:38.820
And now comes the really fun
part of working with Keras--

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.690
creating our model.

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:44.250
Keras supplies a really
easy and intuitive way

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:48.684
to build up your model
from the ground up.

00:04:48.684 --> 00:04:50.850
In this case, we're going
to make a sequential model

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:53.630
and add layers on top of it.

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:56.810
The first letter we'll
have has 30 nodes

00:04:56.810 --> 00:05:00.440
and has an activation function
of a rectified linear unit

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:04.070
for relu, which in the case of
TensorFlow that we used before,

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.800
was the default
activation function.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.780
Then we'll have another
fully-connected layer

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:12.980
or dense layer with
20 neurons, this time

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:15.080
also with a relu function.

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.710
And finally, we'll do our
final mapping to the 10 output

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:22.070
values of zero through
nine and have an activation

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:25.400
of the softmax, which
basically just distributes

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:29.686
power probabilities
across the 10 buckets.

00:05:29.686 --> 00:05:32.580
And now we're ready
to compile our model.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.240
Keras uses this
notation of compiling

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:38.240
a model as similar to
when you do something

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:41.090
like string builder or
something to just say, I'm done.

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:42.560
Put it all together for me.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.950
And we'll supply a loss,
optimizer, and metrics

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:49.970
for what kind of values we
want to get out of it, for how

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:53.930
to optimize for the
best values, as well as

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:55.470
how we want to measure loss.

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:58.940
In this case, we're using
categorical cross entropy

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:01.100
because our outputs
are categorical.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:03.680
And cross entropy, in this
case, happens to be a nice way

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.170
to measure our loss or error.

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:11.230
With our model created,
we're ready to run training.

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:15.040
Training with Keras is
as easy as calling .fit.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.290
When we call .fit, all
we need to supply are

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:20.080
the training_features
and training_labels.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:23.630
It's also a good idea to
supply epochs and batch_size

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:25.900
so that we can control the
training a little more.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.270
In this case, we have
supplied an epoch

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:31.210
of two, which means we'll go
through the entire dataset

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:32.440
twice over.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:35.140
And we'll supply a
batch_size of 128.

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:37.720
This means that with
each training step,

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:41.380
the model will see 128
examples which will help guide

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:43.880
it to adjust its parameters.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:46.870
And so we can see here Keras
has some really useful helpless

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:49.180
as the training
happens and gives us

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:50.680
a sense of the progress.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:53.140
It also then prints out
the loss and accuracy

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:56.610
at the end of each epoch.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:07:00.600
But seeing the accuracy at the
end loss at the end of training

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:03.810
isn't nearly as
useful as evaluation.

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:07.380
We need to see the accuracy
against our actual test

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:08.160
dataset.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:12.020
So let's call our
model.evaluate function

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:15.630
and this time pass in our
test_features and test_labels.

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:18.120
This will give us an
accuracy, and we can

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.010
print that out and take a look.

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:23.650
We can see we got
84.7% accuracy.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:25.110
And of course, we
could certainly

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.750
do better than that
with increased epochs,

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:31.170
a more sophisticated model,
and other approaches.

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:33.120
But this is just
an intro to Keras.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:36.630
And hopefully, this will give
you a good starting point

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:38.670
to start playing
around with Keras

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:42.880
and seeing all
that Keras can do.

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:45.460
Keras has an amazing
community and lots

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:47.750
of samples which,
when you combine

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:49.420
with Kaggle's
community, gives you

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:53.710
a truly epic set of resources to
get you started the right way.

